special_abilities:
  - name: "The Art of Conjuring: Objects"
    description: "Pictures the thing, summons the energy, and it appears in hand or beside him. Simple objects (a chair, rope, cloak, shoes, a sack of marbles) and items with a few moving parts (scissors, a fishing pole, a wagon, a trumpet), plus simple weapons and armor (sword, spear, bow and string, ball and chain, shield, gauntlets, helmet) and old revolvers and black powder guns. Nothing needing an energy source: no energy weapons, Vibro-Blades, modern guns, grenades, computers, radios, E-clips, batteries or engines. He must know the item; a firing revolver or any intricate object needs firsthand knowledge or a full schematic. Maximum weight 40 lbs (18 kg) +2 lbs (1 kg) per level. Moving or specialized parts: three at level one, +1 at levels 2, 4, 6, 8, 10, 12 and 14. Small simple items come up to 12 at a time (arrows, bolts, sling bullets, nails, needles); bullets, darts, throwing stars and large spikes six at a time; shoes, boots, gloves and gauntlets in pairs; every weapon, even a dagger, is one conjuring."
  - name: "The Art of Conjuring: Animals"
    description: "Any real animal or insect he is familiar with (seen or studied in person, on film, in pictures or diagrams), bigger than a housefly and no heavier than 1000 lbs (450 kg). Average statistics; it obeys only the Conjurer, even to its death, but there is no link: he cannot see through its eyes. One animal at a time. No microscopic life, plants, sentient beings (humans, mutant animals, entities, demons, dragons) or a specific animal such as someone''s pet or familiar. It must appear where it can survive and never in mid-air - it appears in his hand or at his feet."
  - name: "The Cost of Conjuring"
    description: "Weapons: small and simple (short sword, knife, blackjack, six darts or throwing stars) 10 P.P.E.; medium and simple (broadsword, spear, morning star, mace, 12 arrows or 6 bullets) 15; large and simple (claymore, pole arm, lance, giant-sized weapons) 25; revolver or black powder gun 50. Objects: small and simple, no moving parts, under 10 lbs (ball, bowl, pot, sack, gloves, 12 needles, 20 ft of rope) 5; small with 1-2 moving parts (scissors, pliers, yo-yo, handcuffs) 10; medium under 25 lbs (chair, light table, bunk, saddle, pants, jacket, 8 ft of chain) 12; medium with 1-2 moving parts (fishing pole, vice, hedge cutters) 22; large and simple, 30+ lbs (plow, table, couch, trunk, robe or cloak, a 4 ft steel pole, a plank) 35; large with 1-2 moving parts (pulley, bicycle, spinning wheel) 50. Add 5 P.P.E. per extra moving or specialized part. Add 30 P.P.E. to make it M.D.C. material: M.D.C. is half the object''s usual S.D.C. (a 50 S.D.C. suit of armor has 25 M.D.C.). M.D.C. weapons in hands with robot or supernatural strength add 1D4 M.D. (small), 1D6 (medium), 2D4 (large or giant), or 1 M.D. for arrows and bullets. Animals by hit points: 10 or less 15 P.P.E.; 11-30 25; 31-50 40; over 50 75."
  - name: "Duration of Conjurings"
    description: "A conjured object or animal lasts one hour per level of the Conjurer, until destroyed, or until he dismisses it at will. Everything he has conjured vanishes if he is knocked unconscious, falls asleep or is slain. From 6th level he may make a creation permanent for 70 P.P.E. (object) or 100 P.P.E. (animal) plus a permanent 1D6 from his own P.P.E. base, which is not regained if the thing is lost. He takes no harm when a conjured thing is destroyed."
  - name: "Sense Ley Lines and Magic Energy"
    description: "As the Ley Line Walker''s ability (#1 under Line Walker Abilities, Rifts RPG p.83)."
  - name: "Limited Spell Knowledge"
    description: "Casts Lantern Light, Globe of Daylight, Fool''s Gold, Ignite Fire, Water to Wine, Purification (food & water), Spoil (food & water) and Superhuman Strength, plus one creation spell (a spell whose name starts with Create) at every other level starting at level two. Cannot perform ritual or circle magic and cannot learn other spells; has only a rudimentary grasp of traditional spell casting."
  - name: "P.P.E."
    description: "A living battery for spells and conjurings: 2D4x10 plus P.E., +2D6 per level. Draws on ley lines as the Ley Line Walker does, but not from people or rituals. Recovers 5 P.P.E. per hour of rest or sleep, 10 per hour of meditation."
restrictions:
  - "Spell strength: +1 at levels four, eight and twelve (not a sheet bonus)."
  - "+5 on initiative (instead of +3) when conjuring something quickly or before an opponent attacks."
  - "Cannot perform ritual or circle magic, and cannot learn spells beyond those listed."
  - "Cybernetics: starts with none and avoids augmentation because it interferes with magic; prosthetics are considered if necessary."
extraction_notes: |
  - Rifts World Book 16: Federation of Magic printed 82-86 (cache p083-p087,
    page_offset 1, text layer). The heading and introduction are printed 82,
    the conjuring rules and cost tables printed 83-84 (read as prose into
    special_abilities), the other abilities and the O.C.C. block printed 85,
    money and cybernetics printed 86.
  - XP: the Conjurer & Controller Magus ladder on printed 160, read off a
    render; it chains at every level.
  - GROUP: magic. The book files all eight O.C.C.s under one heading, O.C.C.s
    of the Federation of Magic (printed 69-70 in the contents). No S.D.C.
    formula is printed, so men_of_arms: false.
  - MAGIC: the eight listed spells are granted by name. Purification and Spoil
    are the catalog rows (the book adds "(food & water)"); superhuman strength
    is Superhuman Strength. The creation spell at levels 2, 4, 6, 8, 10, 12
    and 14 draws from a named list of the catalog''s general Create spells:
    Create Wood, Create Water, Create Bread & Milk, Create Steel and Create
    Magic Scroll. Create Mummy, Create Zombie and Create Golem are left out
    because they are rituals and the Conjurer cannot perform ritual magic;
    Create Arrows is a shaman tradition spell; the level-0 Create rows belong
    to other traditions.
  - BONUSES: save vs magic is stored as spell_magic and ritual_magic, the
    Mystic''s convention. Horror Factor +1 at 3, 6, 9, 11, 13 and 14 as
    printed. Spell strength and the conditional +5 initiative are prose.
  - SKILLS: American as Language: Native Tongue 98 and Literacy: Native
    Language 40+20. Basic & Advanced Math as the two Mathematics rows at
    45+20. Armorer is Field Armorer & Munitions Expert 40+5. Lore:
    Cattle/Animals is Lore: Cattle & Animals.
  - MONEY: 2D4x1000 credits. The 2D4x1000 in black market goods is not coin
    and stays in the body.
  - EQUIPMENT: the automatic pistol or sub-machinegun (player''s choice), the
    extra ammunition clips and the vehicle are in the body; no single catalog
    row stands for an unnamed automatic pistol. 100 ft of light cord is one
    cord entry.
---

## Lore

Like Shifters and Line Walkers, the Conjurer is a wielder of magic, but a very
specialized one. The bulk of his power is the ability to create objects and
animals out of thin air. Because he understands mechanics and conjures simple
devices with moving parts, people sometimes mistake him for a Techno-Wizard,
but nothing a Conjurer makes has any magical power of its own.

Conjuring is a new school, only 30-50 years old and born in the Magic Zone
(some Elves insist it is a rediscovered art from the Palladium World''s Time of
a Thousand Magicks). Conjurers are rarer than almost any other practitioner of
magic. Traditional mages call the art unproven, too narrow and wasteful, and
say a Conjurer is easy prey at long range. Conjurers enjoy being underestimated:
they tend to be cocky, mischievous, resourceful and eager to prove themselves,
most often anarchist or unprincipled.

## Alignment and Race

Alignment: no restriction, but most lean selfish - Unprincipled, Anarchist and
Miscreant. Race: none, though most (85%) are human; men and women alike become
Conjurers. A high P.P. helps but is not required.

## Equipment and Money

Weapons: a survival knife, a hand axe, an automatic pistol or sub-machinegun
(player''s choice), an energy rifle and several extra ammunition clips, plus
whatever the character conjures as needed. Conjurers can use Techno-Wizard
items. Light armor is preferred because heavy armor slows them down.

The vehicle of choice is usually a living animal, such as a horse or fury
beetle, but a motorcycle, dune buggy or hover cycle is also common.

Money: 2D4x1000 in universal credits and 2D4x1000 in black market goods. Most
ordinary things can be conjured as needed.

## GM Notes

Conjurers are freewheeling, risk-taking, cunning characters who feel they have
something to prove; many love adventure and fame, and like to pit their magic
and wits against other practitioners of magic and supernatural beings.
', 'published', 'data-script'
WHERE NOT EXISTS (SELECT 1 FROM imported_classes WHERE class_id = 'conjurer');


-- Read the result back rather than trusting the exit code. d1-apply prints
-- these, and a CR in the stored markdown means the checkout mangled the file.
SELECT class_id, name, status, length(markdown) AS bytes, instr(markdown, char(13)) > 0 AS has_cr
  FROM imported_classes WHERE class_id = 'conjurer';

